<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#kahuna">Glossary</a> · Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kahuna</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as kahuna nui, kahuna lapaʻau</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kahuna is a Hawaiian word for an expert in any field, used historically for doctors, surgeons and dentists as well as priests, ministers and sorcerers, and for masters of crafts such as canoe building, navigation and carving; it is glossed as 'keeper of hidden knowledge', from kahu, 'caretaker', and huna, 'secret'. According to Fornander the priesthood had ten branches, and a master of all ten could become kahuna nui, high priest, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hewahewa">Hewahewa</a> was to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-i">Kamehameha I</a>. Some kahuna practices were outlawed from 1831 to 1863 and from 1887 to 1919; since then all have been legal except sorcery, which was decriminalised in 1972.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kahuna.jpg" alt="Kahuna" width="330" height="257" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hawaiian_Kahuna_Kahiko_(PP-33-11-023).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Priests and experts (<b>kahuna</b>) served the chiefs, the commoners (makaʻāinana) farmed and fished for them all, and the outcast kauwā stood below everyone.</blockquote><a href="/societies/hawaii">Social Hierarchy of Hawaii under the Kapu</a></li><li><blockquote>Priests (<b>kahuna</b>) of the Hawaiian religion recited the Kumulipo during the makahiki season, which honoured the god Lono.</blockquote><a href="/songs/kumulipo">Kumulipo</a></li></ul></section>
